## 9. Buffs and debuffs

Eight setters, one per (stat, direction, duration). All take `TIER`.

```paradox
POD_combat_move_set_soak_buff_round    = { TIER = 1 }
POD_combat_move_set_soak_buff_combat   = { TIER = 2 }
POD_combat_move_set_soak_debuff_round  = { TIER = 1 }
POD_combat_move_set_soak_debuff_combat = { TIER = 3 }
POD_combat_move_set_hit_buff_round     = { TIER = 1 }
POD_combat_move_set_hit_buff_combat    = { TIER = 2 }
POD_combat_move_set_hit_debuff_round   = { TIER = 1 }
POD_combat_move_set_hit_debuff_combat  = { TIER = 3 }
```

| `TIER` | Size | Magnitude |
|---|---|---|
| `1` | small | 10 |
| `2` | medium | 20 |
| `3` | large | 30 |

`round` clears next turn; `combat` lasts the fight. Reapplication is
upgrade-only: a higher tier overwrites a lower one, equal or lower is blocked.
One buff and one debuff can coexist per stat and duration. Do not balance
repeated use as stacking numerically.

Soak tiers are the single store the wound roll reads: PoD's `POD_soak_chance`
reads the bridge variables directly, and combat keeps no second copy.

---

## 10. Tempo

```paradox
POD_combat_move_set_ap_gain           = { VALUE = 1 }
POD_combat_move_set_ap_drain          = { VALUE = 2 }
POD_combat_move_set_initiative_debuff = { VALUE = 10 }
```

`ap_gain` affects the actor. `ap_drain` and `initiative_debuff` affect the
selected target and never broadcast.

Repeated AP drains keep the **largest pending** drain rather than adding.
Initiative debuffs **do** add within a round. A drain of 6 empties a standard
budget: positive-cost moves become unaffordable, but 0-AP moves stay usable.

Any move capable of a net-positive AP loop needs a real resource cost and a
repeat limit.

---

## 11. Retaliation

```paradox
POD_combat_move_set_retaliate_damage = { VALUE = @damage_minor }
POD_combat_move_set_flag = { NAME = retaliate_if_hit }
```

Optional typing, both parameterless:

```paradox
POD_combat_move_set_retaliate_agg    = yes
POD_combat_move_set_retaliate_morale = yes
```

| Arming flag | Fires on |
|---|---|
| `retaliate_if_hit` | an incoming HP-damage move that makes contact, including fully soaked or guard-blocked |
| `retaliate_if_missed` | an incoming HP-damage move that misses or is evaded |
| `retaliate_if_attacked` | either outcome, plus one shrugged off by immunity |

Contact and miss are disjoint, so pairing the first two arms **two independent
charges**. When a specific charge and the always-charge are both armed, the
specific one answers and the always-charge is held, so at most one counter
fires per incoming attack.

Rearming compares **amount only, within one charge family**: a stronger plain
counter overwrites a weaker aggravated one.

Counter kind precedence: morale > aggravated > the move's elemental class >
plain.

Retaliation only fires against an incoming move that carries HP damage, and
never against a `team_enemy` or `team_all` move. A pure morale attack, a
control move, a heal or a DoT-only move never triggers an armed counter.

The counter skips the contact resolver entirely, so the spirit gate is
hand-rolled: a physical counter against an incorporeal target no-ops unless the
retaliator can damage spirits.

---

## 12. Damage over time

```paradox
POD_combat_move_set_dot  = { AMOUNT = 1 ROUNDS = 2 }
POD_combat_move_set_flag = { NAME = dot_morale }
```

Both `AMOUNT` and `ROUNDS` are raw integers with no constant family. Ticks
resolve at round end.

Tick kind: superficial by default, the move's aggravated or elemental class
when present, morale when `dot_morale` is set.

- Use `TARGETING = single_enemy`.
- Orthogonal to direct damage: a move can strike and burn.
- Reapplying **refreshes**, it does not stack.
- A guard-blocked or fully soaked contact still seeds it. A miss, dodge,
  stealth evade or spirit-gate failure does not.
- HP ticks bypass guard and soak once applied, and can knock a target out
  between turns.

---

## 13. Summons

```paradox
POD_combat_move_set_summon = { RECIPE = wraith }
```

`RECIPE` is `wraith`, `bane`, `gaian_spirit` or `elemental`. The setter writes
both `summons_combatant` and `summon_recipe`.

Add `POD_combat_move_set_flag = { NAME = summon_hostile }` to seat the creature
on the side opposite the caster. No shipped move does this, so treat that path
as unexercised.

Author with `TARGETING = self`, no HP damage, a meaningful supernatural cost,
and `POD_combat_move_set_once_per = { DURATION = combat }`.

The summon sits out the cast round, acts from the next round, is knocked out
rather than killed, and is cleaned up at combat end. An AI summon is given a
full loadout at seat time, or it would seat and then do nothing.

---

## 14. Outcome riders

Three payloads can be gated on the attack result.

```paradox
POD_combat_move_set_heal_condition          = { VALUE = on_hp_damage }
POD_combat_move_set_morale_damage_condition = { VALUE = on_contact }
POD_combat_move_set_self_damage_condition   = { VALUE = on_miss }
```

| `VALUE` | Fires when |
|---|---|
| `always` | unconditionally. This is the default, so omit the setter |
| `on_hp_damage` | at least one wound box landed after soak |
| `on_contact` | the move landed, including fully soaked or guard-blocked |
| `on_miss` | the move missed or was evaded |

Morale healing has no rider and is always unconditional.

A life-drain heal should use `on_hp_damage`, not `on_contact`: a fully soaked
strike drew no vitality.

These read the HP/contact result axes. A morale-only move never establishes
them, so a non-`always` rider on one is meaningless.

---

## 15. Conditions

One condition per move, used as a gate **or** as a bonus, never both.

```paradox
POD_combat_move_set_condition = {
    MODE      = gate
    TARGET    = target
    PREDICATE = hp_missing
    THRESHOLD = 2
}
```

| Parameter | Values |
|---|---|
| `MODE` | `gate` (move unusable while false) or `bonus` (always usable, extra effect when true) |
| `TARGET` | `user`, `target`, `global`, `relational` |
| `PREDICATE` | one of the 25 below |
| `THRESHOLD` | the number for value predicates; pass `0` for boolean ones |

### Predicates

| Predicate | `TARGET` | `THRESHOLD` |
|---|---|---|
| `first_round` | `global` | 0 |
| `round_min` | `global` | minimum round |
| `outnumbered` | `global` | 0 |
| `ally_knocked_out` | `global` | 0 |
| `alive_enemies_min` | `global` | minimum living enemies |
| `alive_enemies_max` | `global` | maximum living enemies |
| `alive_allies_min` | `global` | minimum living allies **excluding the actor** |
| `alive_allies_max` | `global` | maximum living allies **excluding the actor** (0 = last one standing) |
| `undamaged` | `user` or `target` | 0 |
| `hp_missing` | `user` or `target` | minimum wound boxes filled |
| `full_morale` | `user` or `target` | 0 |
| `morale_missing` | `user` or `target` | minimum morale lost |
| `has_guard` | `user` or `target` | 0 |
| `has_dodge` | `user` or `target` | 0 |
| `has_stealth` | `user` or `target` | 0 |
| `prestige_level_min` | `user` or `target` | 0-5 |
| `piety_level_min` | `user` or `target` | 0-5 |
| `dread_min` | `user` or `target` | 0-100 |
| `has_weapon_artifact` | `user` or `target` | 0 |
| `has_armor_artifact` | `user` or `target` | 0 |
| `relation_positive` | `relational` | minimum opinion |
| `relation_negative` | `relational` | minimum opinion magnitude, as a positive number |
| `has_trait` | `user` or `target` | 0, plus `PARAM` |
| `has_modifier` | `user` or `target` | 0, plus `PARAM` |
| `has_perk` | `user` or `target` | 0, plus `PARAM` |
| `fera_form` | `user` or `target` | 0, plus `PARAM` |
| `target_known_secret` | `relational` | 0, plus `PARAM` |

A value predicate with `THRESHOLD = 0` is always true. That is a design bug,
not a feature.

`target_known_secret` has an empty dispatcher and always fails. Do not ship a
move on it.

### Identifier predicates

CK3 cannot interpolate a variable into `has_trait`, `has_character_modifier` or
`has_perk`, so those predicates carry a literal key and each has a sub-dispatcher in
`common/scripted_triggers/POD_combat/pod_combat_condition_dispatch_triggers.txt`.
`fera_form` joins them because it resolves to a differently-named trigger per form.

```paradox
POD_combat_move_set_cond_param = { PARAM = vampire }
```

That whole file is **generated** from the `cond_param` values live CSV rows use,
so a dispatcher emits a case only for a key some move actually carries:

| Predicate | Keys the dispatcher resolves today | What a key may be |
|---|---|---|
| `has_trait` | `vampire`, `wraith`, `spirit`, `psychic` | any trait key |
| `has_perk` | `combat_healing_perk`, `strength_of_purpose_perk` | any perk in PoD's `lifestyle_perks` |
| `fera_form` | `warform` | `primalhuman`, `warform`, `primalbeast`, `beast` |
| `has_modifier` | none (fail-closed stub) | an alias on the `COND_READABLE_MODIFIERS` allow-list |
| `target_known_secret` | none (fail-closed stub) | any secret_type |

The last column is what the CSV *may* name; the middle column is what is emitted
today. A stub means no live row uses that predicate yet - not that the predicate
is broken. To use another key, add the CSV row and regenerate; never hand-edit
the dispatch file, since the next run discards it. **Unknown predicates and
unknown params fail closed**, so a typo is a condition that is simply never true.

A new literal flag may also need a row in
`pod_combat_error_suppression_effects.txt`, but only after spelling, scope and
real runtime use are verified.

